  "worked_solution": "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\u27e8\\cdot ,\\cdot \u27e9$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\u2016p(t)\u2016=\\sqrt{\u27e8p(t),p(t)\u27e9}.\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)={t}^{2}-3t+4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=2,\\,p(2)=2,\\,p(3)=4\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8p(t),p(t)\u27e9 & =[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =4+4+16 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016p(t)\u2016 & =\\sqrt{24}=2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $p(t)$, we divide $p(t)$ by its norm $\u2016p(t)\u2016:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{p(t)}{\u2016p(t)\u2016} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}-3t+4}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{12}({t}^{2}-3t+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
